{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/failed-hypothesis-reset.md", "content": "# Resetting after failed ARC hypotheses

Repeated incorrect outputs indicate the task class or answer representation may be wrong, not merely a coordinate detail. Before spending another attempt, revisit all demonstrations: list input/output dimensions, identify separators and partitions, enumerate each region's candidate features, and test a compact region-summary mapping across every pair. Do not submit a revised pattern unless it exactly reproduces all known demonstrations. A new demo request should target a clearly stated unresolved distinction; serial requests without analysis impose cost without improving inference.

For small outputs from large partitioned canvases, prioritize understanding the relationship between each cell/region and the compact answer. Keep a candidate mapping table and explicitly account for exceptions (e.g. output cells retaining a background/category color). Only after the mapping reproduces all demos should it be applied to the test.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-3/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/failed-hypothesis-reset.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-stream-solving", "file": "references/output-dimension-pitfall.md", "content": "# Compact outputs are evidence

Before inferring an ARC transformation, compare input and demonstration-output dimensions. A smaller output is a strong signal that the task may extract or encode a property, rather than paint a full-size grid. Do not assume a template-stamping task solely because the input contains separators, repeating blocks, or sparse colored markers.

Derive the output structure from the examples: identify repeated tiles/regions, inspect how the sparse markers relate spatially to each region, and map the demonstrated selection or encoding to the compact output. Validate every output cell against the inputs. If multiple mappings still fit, request a demo chosen to distinguish those hypotheses; never submit an arbitrary placeholder based on an unsupported visual guess.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/held-out-3/play/users/u012/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/continual-arc-stream-solving/references/output-dimension-pitfall.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/demo-output-mapping.md", "content": "# Validate the demo's output mapping before transfer

A visually structured demo output is not evidence by itself for reflection, tiling, or stamping. After the first plausible but incorrect submission, reopen the task class and output representation before changing coordinates. Record input/output dimensions, map input rows, columns, objects, or symbols to output cells/regions, and compare that candidate mapping against every available pair. Treat exact full-demo reproduction as a prerequisite for submitting; a coherent pattern that fails even one demo is still an unvalidated guess. If requesting another demo, identify which competing mappings its predicted output would distinguish.
